# Burhan production reverse proxy.
#
# SECURITY (audit 2026-10-23): the previous configuration exposed a fully
# unauthenticated SSRF relay — any client could pass ?XTransformPort=<port>
# and reach ANY service bound to localhost (databases, admin panels, the
# engine, metrics). Port/host selection from untrusted request input has
# been REMOVED ENTIRELY. The upstream is now a static, compile-time target.
#
# QA (2026-10-23): comments converted from '//' to '#' — Caddyfile syntax
# only supports '#' comments; the '//' style made this file UNLOADABLE
# ("unrecognized directive: //"), so a deployment with the "fixed" config
# would have failed to start caddy at all.
#
# Deployment rules that accompany this file:
#   * the Next.js app (:3000) is the ONLY upstream reachable through :81;
#   * internal services (engine, fixture app on 127.0.0.1:8777, Prisma,
#     anything else) MUST bind to loopback and MUST NOT be exposed by any
#     other proxy rule or port mapping;
#   * do not reintroduce query/header-driven upstream selection;
#   * the app trusts forwarded IP headers ONLY together with the shared
#     proxy secret below (item 1.4) — keep PROXY_SECRET (Caddy env) and
#     TRUSTED_PROXY_SECRET (app env) identical: openssl rand -hex 32.
:81 {
        handle {
                reverse_proxy localhost:3000 {
                        header_up Host {host}
                        header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
                        header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
                        header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
                        # PROXY-SECRET GATE (owner order 2026-10-31, item 1.4):
                        # without it, any DIRECT client could forge
                        # X-Forwarded-For and buy a fresh per-IP rate-limit
                        # bucket per request (proven: 130 distinct forged
                        # values, zero refusals against a 120/hour budget).
                        header_up X-Proxy-Secret {$PROXY_SECRET}
                }
        }
}
